Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Quality estimation framework.}
    Structural agreement signals reliably predict output quality for LLM-based NER: VC achieves $\rho{=}0.38$ on the medium-difficulty primary test bed (SciERC), while EM reaches AUROC${=}0.82$ on CoNLL.
    Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent but stable across model families and a 4B scale ablation, supporting practical applications such as selective prediction and error flagging.

    \item \textbf{Diagnosis of the correlation-selection gap.}
    The gap operates at two layers.
    \textit{Definitional}: four of five signals are instance-level aggregates that assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates; consensus-proximity (MBR) conversion yields per-sample rankings but does not recover selection ability.
    \textit{Empirical}: two mechanisms jointly eliminate residual discriminability---(a) instance degeneracy (21--58\% of instances produce identical F1 across all $N$ samples, scaling with model capability; on non-degenerate instances, consensus-proximity closes 23--41\% of the oracle gap) and (b) LP compression (median within-instance range 0.041 nats, $\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){=}0.248$; on the non-tied subset, LP selection gains $+2.9$~pp, $p{=}0.004$).
    A LoRA-rank ablation reduces degeneracy from 21\% to 7\% without enabling selection, confirming that low degeneracy is necessary but not sufficient.

    \item \textbf{Diagnostic ablation chain.}
    A five-layer ablation (free-form decoding, entity-token LP, verbalized confidence, non-tied subset, signal combinations) confirms these mechanisms are intrinsic to capable fine-tuned models, not artifacts of constrained decoding or signal design. Signal rankings shift with task difficulty and collapse at the capability boundary; closing the gap likely requires learned per-sample scoring.
\end{enumerate}

