Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Quality estimation framework.}
    We systematically evaluate five confidence signals from three families---structural, surface, and model-internal---for LLM-based NER across a difficulty gradient (three benchmarks, two model families, a 4B scale ablation).
    Structural signals reliably predict output quality (VC $\rho{=}0.38$ on the primary test bed; EM AUROC${=}0.82$ on CoNLL), with difficulty-dependent but model-stable rankings that support selective prediction and error flagging.

    \item \textbf{Diagnosis of the correlation-selection gap.}
    Quality estimation does not translate to best-of-$N$ selection: despite 13--19~pp oracle headroom, no signal significantly outperforms greedy decoding on the primary test bed ($p{>}0.05$), a gap confirmed by bootstrap testing at $N{=}32$.
    We trace this to two mechanisms---instance degeneracy (21--58\% of instances yield identical outputs, scaling with model capability) and LP compression (median within-instance range 0.041 nats)---and confirm via a diagnostic chain (LoRA-rank ablation, free-form decoding, entity-token LP, supervised signal combination) that the bottleneck lies in the feature space, not signal aggregation or decoding constraints.
    The gap is regime-dependent: most pronounced at moderate performance, with marginal exception at ceiling and signal collapse at the low-resource boundary.
\end{enumerate}

