\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) provides an informative contrast: at matched difficulty (greedy F1${=}0.647$, comparable to SciERC's $0.645$) with substantial oracle headroom ($10.7$~pp), LP achieves significant selection gains ($+1.17$~pp, 95\% CI $[+1.02, +1.34]$) while all structural signals degrade selection (SJ $-1.95$, FK/EM $-3.33$, VC $-1.54$~pp).

\paragraph{Why structural signals fail on Few-NERD.}
Few-NERD's 8 coarse entity types exhibit substantial semantic overlap (e.g., \textit{organization}, \textit{building}, \textit{event} share named entities), producing systematic type confusion: multiple samples may agree on entity spans but disagree on types.
Structural consistency thus reflects inter-sample agreement on a \textit{shared misclassification pattern} rather than correctness.
VC achieves the highest quality estimation (global $\rho{=}0.52$) yet the worst-performing selection signal ($-1.54$~pp), a direct manifestation of the correlation-selection gap in the type-confusion regime.

\paragraph{Why LP succeeds.}
LP does not depend on inter-sample structural agreement; it reflects model uncertainty at the token level.
On Few-NERD, LP's conditional within-instance correlation ($\rho{=}0.23$) provides sufficient per-sample discriminability to select better outputs, consistent with the non-tied subset finding on SciERC (\S\ref{sec:discussion}): where LP compression is less severe, selection works.
Few-NERD's large instance count ($n{=}37{,}648$) and 17.4\% zero-F1 rate (vs.\ SciERC's ${\approx}$4\%) suggest that the LP signal is concentrated in instances where the model is genuinely uncertain about entity boundaries, not merely type labels.

